# Design Contract

Use the pinned DenchCo visual adapter as the baseline, then record only deliberate target-specific token and interaction decisions here. Navigation and synthesis must follow the target's audience and governing question.

Record how the confirmed accent generated its light, dark, visited, transparent, and contrast tokens. Verify at least 4.5:1 contrast for normal accent-coloured text and 3:1 for non-text interactive boundaries against their rendered backgrounds.

Every displayed governing-question callout must expose the `governing-question` marker and use the active accent colour for both its inline-start rail and question text at every supported viewport. Ordinary quotations remain neutral.

When primary navigation exposes a page's `draft` status, adapt the pinned Pen Circle marker and “Draft — research in progress” tooltip with the complete renderer asset closure. Align the marker's trailing centre with the open/close chevron column in the supported desktop navigation. Other lifecycle states keep distinct semantics and artwork.

When the Human Wiki uses Mermaid, adapt the pinned semantic diagram roles (`kb-source`, `kb-evidence`, `kb-snapshot`, `kb-normative`, `kb-canonical`, `kb-product`, `kb-derived`, `kb-consumer`, and `kb-verification`) through the complete renderer dependency closure. Use the target accent family, preserve accessible titles and descriptions, and distinguish derived surfaces from authority. Entry and architecture diagrams use a top-to-bottom reading direction, straight connectors, short labels, no edge labels, no more than five nodes and five visible relationships, and at most one parallel branch. Move finer detail into nearby prose or a separate diagram. Connectors must not intersect text and nodes must not overlap; verify the rendered result at desktop, tablet, and mobile widths.
